    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "The architect's studio is where buildings begin — as ideas sketched on tracing paper, developed into digital models, and refined through countless iterations before a single brick is laid. Modern studios blend traditional craftsmanship with cutting-edge technology: drawing boards sit alongside powerful workstations running CAD (Computer-Aided Design) and BIM (Building Information Modelling) software. Physical scale models — meticulously assembled from card, foam, and laser-cut acrylic — share shelf space with 3D-printed prototypes. The studio culture is collaborative: architects, structural engineers, landscape designers, and sustainability consultants work together from the earliest concept stages. The design process typically follows RIBA (Royal Institute of British Architects) work stages — from strategic definition and concept design through to technical design, construction, and post-occupancy evaluation. Pinned to the walls are mood boards, site photographs, material samples, and precedent studies — visual references that inform the design direction. Client presentations happen in the studio or on-site, with architects using renders (photorealistic digital images) and fly-through animations to help clients visualise the finished building. The profession demands a rigorous education: in the UK, qualifying as an architect requires seven years of study and professional experience — a three-year undergraduate degree, a two-year postgraduate diploma, and two years of monitored professional practice, culminating in the Part 3 examination.",
